The complete link needs more than glass. Lasers produce light; modulators can encode data onto it; detectors read it. Control electronics and, in some designs, digital signal processors help operate the link and recover the data.

Those parts require power, cooling, packaging and testing.

An active optical cable, or AOC, has its optical ends permanently attached to the fiber. Pluggable transceivers are removable modules that send and receive light through separate fiber. An AOC is one assembly; pluggables allow a module or fiber to be replaced separately.

4. Same speed, different reach

Imagine copper and optical links both rated at 400 Gb/s: 400 billion bits per second. Their advertised line rate—the rate of bits traveling over the link—is the same.

Fiber's advantage here is maintaining a readable signal over a longer path. It does not automatically increase the data rate or make the application faster.

At short distances, passive copper often offers low cost and low power. Active copper can extend the workable distance. Optics becomes attractive when reach or cabling density makes copper impractical.

There is no single distance cutoff for every product. The exact lane rate, cable, connectors and endpoint hardware determine the choice.

5. Try a concrete design

Question: A cluster needs 64 links over an 18-meter cable route. Each link is rated at 400 Gb/s. Approved passive copper reaches 3 meters; an approved AOC reaches 30 meters and uses 12 watts for both ends combined. Which works, and what power does it add?

All numbers are teaching assumptions, not universal product specifications.

The 18-meter route exceeds copper's approved 3-meter reach. The 30-meter AOC works.

The combined line rate in one direction remains:

64 × 400 Gb/s = 25,600 Gb/s = 25.6 Tb/s.

The cable assemblies consume:

64 × 12 W = 768 W = 0.768 kW.

If powered continuously for a 365-day year, their energy use is:

0.768 kW × 8,760 hours = 6,728 kWh.

Watts measure how quickly energy is being used. Kilowatt-hours measure the total energy used over time.

Optics makes these connections possible at the required distance. It does not increase their rated speed. The 768 watts excludes the rest of the network interfaces, switches and cooling.

6. Test whether the cable is the problem

Suppose long copper links show bit errors—received bits differ from those sent—or repeatedly disconnect as temperature rises.

Keep the workload and other hardware the same, then try a qualified optical connection. If errors fall and the job speeds up, the electrical signal path was likely part of the problem.

If errors disappear but the job stays slow, examine the next layer: crowded switch ports, poorly coordinated communication or slow computation.

Light is not a magic shortcut for delay, either. Delay includes putting bits onto the link, traveling along it, passing through switches and waiting in queues.

7. Why the choice matters beyond the cable

Copper requires good conductors, shielding, connectors and assembly. Optics adds specialized light-producing and light-detecting components, packaging and testing.

A missing qualified optical module can prevent an otherwise ready rack from connecting to the cluster. Yet using optics on every short connection can add unnecessary cost and power.

Choose a reliable connection that meets the actual distance, speed and servicing requirements. NVIDIA's LinkX range includes several copper and optical options because one design does not fit every connection.

Remember

- Copper sends electrical signals; fiber carries light between electrical endpoints.
- More distance and higher lane rates make copper signaling harder.
- Optics adds reach and can ease cabling, while requiring conversion hardware and power.

